From c2c23b536d012f8199364ce2d501613738ac15f4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 19:22:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(textures): DESIGN-02 - the tile that wrapped by drawing a
 rectangle on itself

Three defects in the tiles DESIGN-01 shipped, found by measuring the thing I
had only looked at.

1. THE SEAM. seamless() faded the whole tile against a half-period roll of
   itself, weighting by an x ramp TIMES a y ramp. The roll is the right idea -
   a cyclic roll by half the length is continuous across the tile boundary by
   construction, because the two columns that meet there were neighbours in the
   middle of the original - but the product of two ramps means the boundary
   between "mostly original" and "mostly rolled" traces a RECTANGLE across the
   middle of the tile. Two different pieces of wood either side of a rectangle
   is a patchwork. It was invisible while every tile repeated eight times
   across a board and unmissable the moment red oak repeated one and a half
   times, which is how it survived being looked at.

   One axis at a time fixes it. Each pass's weight depends on a single
   coordinate, so neither leaves a closed boundary anywhere, and the second
   preserves what the first established: rolling along y cannot break
   periodicity along x, and a blend of two x-periodic images is x-periodic.

   The obvious alternative - cross-fade each edge against the opposite edge -
   is what I wrote first and it does not work. The first column ends up a
   mixture of two columns and the last column a mixture of two DIFFERENT ones,
   so the join is no better than it started. It looked fine in a contact sheet
   and the measurement said 16x.

2. THE SEAM WAS NOT MEASURED AT ALL, and then it was measured wrongly. The
   first metric compared the step across the join against the step between
   neighbouring interior columns. That is the sensitive measurement and it is
   the wrong denominator: grain runs along the tile, so neighbouring ROWS are
   nearly identical - 0.22 grey levels on hard maple - and dividing by that
   turns a seam of 0.87 in 255 into "3.95x", which reads like a failure and is
   below what a display can show. The denominator is now the tile's own
   standard deviation, which is the contrast a person actually sees. The set
   runs 0.02 to 0.13; the script refuses to write anything over 0.25, and the
   neighbour ratio is still recorded because it moves first on a regression.

3. RED OAK WAS CUT THROUGH A WINDOW SHADOW. Widening its crop to get more
   board width - red oak's cathedral figure is board-scale, so a narrow tile
   repeats the same arch twice across every plank - walked it into the shadow
   that crosses the top of that frame. A three-inch flatten divides out a
   window; it does not divide out an edge that sharp, and a dark diagonal
   printed across every plank in the room is a worse defect than a tile that
   repeats. Back below the shadow, and the reason is written next to the crop.

Also: build-grain.py now WRITES the table in lib/floor-studio/grain.ts, between
two markers, instead of a person copying six numbers out of the manifest. The
numbers have to exist twice - the bundle cannot read a JSON file at module
scope and six small integers are not worth a network round trip before the
first floor can be drawn - and two copies of a number are only safe when one of
them is never typed by hand. grain.test.ts still asserts they agree, which now
catches the one remaining way they can drift: editing the generated block
instead of re-running the script.

65 of 66 guards. parse-scan clean. verify:css cannot run without an install.

